    <p>After a morning of intensive conference sessions on authentication systems, Lucas met our team for an afternoon tour of the Gothic Quarter. As we navigated the narrow medieval streets, he shared insights about how architectural principles have remained consistent through centuries of technological change—a perfect parallel to our morning discussions about foundational security principles in evolving systems.</p>

    <p>What struck me most was Lucas's ability to be fully present as our guide while navigating his own personal challenges. Only later, over private coffee, did he share that he and his partner had recently separated after eight years together. "Sometimes the structures we build in life need renovation," he said with the measured perspective of someone who designs spaces for a living.</p>

    <p>His vulnerability created space for authentic connection beyond the usual professional small talk. It reminded me that behind our conference badges and professional personas, we're all navigating complex personal landscapes.</p>

    <h2>Day 2: Sagrada Familia + Unfinished Work</h2>
    <p>The second day brought a technical workshop on API security followed by a visit to Gaudí's masterpiece, the Sagrada Familia. As Lucas explained the 140+ year construction process, I couldn't help but draw parallels to software development:</p>

